{"title": "CSCI-331: Introduction to Artificial Intelligence", "learningObjectives": [
        "Define the core components of the NIST AI Risk Management Framework and explain their roles in governance and risk mitigation.",
        "Explain the fundamental workflow of machine learning, including data preparation, training, validation, and evaluation.",
        "Identify and apply three key technical terms from machine learning workflows to a practical problem-solving scenario."
      ], "beats": [
        {
          "title": "Course Foundations and Scope",
          "claim": "CSCI-331 introduces the theories and algorithms used to create artificial intelligence systems, covering search, logic, planning, machine learning, and applications in computer, 33",
          "example": "Students will explore how AI algorithms power real-world systems like recommendation engines and autonomous navigation.",
          "visualDirection": "Display a slide with the course title, core topics, and a simple diagram linking AI algorithms to application areas.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-331"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Prerequisites and Course Constraints",
          "claim": "Students must complete CSCI-243 or SWEN-262 and MATH-251 or STAT-205, and cannot take CSCI-630 to receive credit.",
          "example": "A student with a background in software engineering and statistics can proceed, while one who completed CSCI-630 must take an alternative path.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a prerequisite flowchart highlighting required courses and the exclusion of CSCI-630.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-331"]
        },
        {
          "title": "NIST AI Risk Management Framework",
          "claim": "The NIST AI Risk Management Framework organizes work around Govern, Map, Measure, and Manage to address AI risk.",
          "example": "Govern establishes policies, Map documents context and impacts, Measure analyzes risks, and Manage prioritizes responses.",
          "visualDirection": "Create a four-step cycle diagram labeled Govern, Map, Measure, Manage with brief labels for each phase.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ai-rmf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Risk Analysis Considerations",
          "claim": "Risk analysis considers data quality, uncertainty, robustness, privacy, security, transparency, and fairness.",
          "example": "In a healthcare AI system, data quality and fairness are critical to ensure accurate diagnoses and equitable outcomes.",
          "visualDirection": "List the seven risk considerations in a circular diagram centered on 'Risk Analysis' to show their interconnected roles.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ai-rmf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Monitoring and Context Changes",
          "claim": "Monitoring continues after deployment because context and system behavior can change over time.",
          "example": "A self-driving car's AI must continuously monitor road conditions and sensor data to adapt to new environments.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a timeline graphic with 'Deployment' followed by an ongoing 'Monitoring' loop with arrows indicating continuous feedback.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ai-rmf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Machine Learning Workflow Overview",
          "claim": "Machine-learning workflows separate data preparation, training, validation, testing, model selection, and evaluation.",
          "example": "Developing a spam filter involves cleaning email data, training a classifier, validating on unseen data, and testing performance.",
          "visualDirection": "Display a linear flowchart with six boxes representing each workflow stage, connected by arrows.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Supervised and Unsupervised Methods",
          "claim": "Supervised methods learn a mapping from examples paired with target labels, while unsupervised methods identify structure without target labels.",
          "example": "Supervised learning trains a model to classify images as cats or dogs, whereas unsupervised learning groups similar images by color patterns.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a side-by-side comparison: left side shows labeled examples for supervised learning, right side shows unlabeled data clustering for unsupervised learning.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Preprocessing and Leakage Prevention",
          "claim": "Preprocessing must be fitted only on permitted training data to avoid leakage, which can invalidate results.",
          "example": "Scaling features using only training data statistics prevents test data information from influencing the model's performance.",
          "visualDirection": "Illustrate a pipeline where 'Preprocessing' is applied only to the 'Training Data' box, with a red 'X' over applying it to test data.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Pipelines and Cross-Validation",
          "claim": "Pipelines combine preprocessing and estimators so the same transformations are applied consistently, and cross-validation repeats training and validation across defined data splits",
          "example": "A single pipeline ensures that image resizing and normalization are applied identically during training and testing, while cross-validation evaluates model stability across multiple data folds.",
          "visualDirection": "Draw a pipeline block containing 'Preprocessing' and 'Estimator' components, with a cross-validation loop showing multiple data splits feeding into the pipeline.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Evaluation Metrics and Hyperparameter Selection",
          "claim": "Evaluation metrics must match the task and the cost of different errors, and hyperparameter selection must remain separate from final test evaluation.",
          "example": "For medical diagnosis, precision and recall are prioritized over accuracy, and hyperparameters are tuned only on validation data before final testing.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a decision matrix matching metrics to task costs, alongside a separate 'Hyperparameter Tuning' box that does not touch the final test set.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        }
      ]}
